      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "Method 1:\n\nThe least common multiple of two integers (the least common multiple of numbers $a$ and $b$) that are relatively prime is always equal to the multiplication of the sum of the two numbers.\n\nSince the greatest common divisor of the two numbers is 6, one of the numbers cannot be a multiple of 3. Therefore, to satisfy the condition that the least common multiple is 36, one of the numbers must be 6 and 6 will be divided by the other.\n\nThe product of two numbers whose product is $36$ is $\\boxed{72}$.\n\n[asy]\nlabel(\"36\",(0,0),S); label(\"6\",(2,0),S); label(\"x\",(0,1),W); label(\"y\",(1,0),S); label(\"$\\mathrm{LCM}=(6a+6b)$\",(0,-.5),S); label(\"$\\mathrm{GCD}=6$\",(1.5,0),S); label(\"$6a=6b$\",(1,-.5),S);\n\ndraw((0,0)--(6,0),dashed);\ndraw((0,1)--(1,0),dashed);\ndraw((0,-.5)--(.75,-.5),dashed);\ndraw((1,0)--(.6,.6),dashed);\ndraw((0,-.5)--(0.5,1.19),dashed);\ndraw((1,0)--(-.6, 1.19),dashed);\ndraw((1,0)--(.75,1.19-.5),dashed);\ndraw((1,0)--(-.6,1.19-.5),dashed);\ndraw((1.5,0)--(-.6,1.19-.5),dashed);[/asy].\n\nThe list of possible solutions is: $\\boxed{72}$",
